    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build passes and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved date-derived values onto a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starting 1 July, identified by the calendar year it ends in \u2014 the Australian convention this app already uses for postcodes/holidays).\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **New helper `FiscalYearDeriver`** (`mapper/FiscalYearDeriver.java`) \u2014 mirrors the existing \"Deriver\" helper pattern. Provides `fiscalYear(date)` (July\u2013Dec \u2192 `year+1`, Jan\u2013Jun \u2192 `year`), `fiscalYearLabel(date)` \u2192 `FY<YY>`, and `elapsedFiscalYears(from, to)`.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 added the `FiscalYearDeriver` import; added a `fiscalYear` mapping returning `FY<YY>` from the (already business-day-adjusted) stored registration date; and changed `membershipNumber`'s `<YY>` segment to use the fiscal year instead of the raw registration-date year.\n\n3. **`MembershipPointsDeriver`** \u2014 the +3 tenure bonus now triggers on *more than one elapsed fiscal year* rather than a 365-day window (dropped the `ChronoUnit.DAYS` check).\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `fiscalYear` property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it), and updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to reflect the fiscal-year basis.\n\nThe new `coreReturnsFiscalYear` test passes, and the audit log confirms correct behaviour: a registration rolled to 2026-08-17 (August \u2265 July) yields fiscal year 2027 \u2192 `FY27` and membership number `\u2026-M27`.\n\nNote: the exact tenure threshold (\"more than 365 days\" \u2192 \"more than one elapsed fiscal year\") is the one spot where the spec left a judgment call; I mapped the original \">1 year\" semantics to \">1 elapsed fiscal year\" and documented it. If you intended a different threshold (e.g. \u22651 fiscal year / any boundary crossing), that's a one-line change in `MembershipPointsDeriver`.",
